<section>
    <h2>One test, many tabs</h2>
    <p>
        Real sites send you elsewhere all the time: a "Pay with…" button opens a provider's window, a help link opens
        in a new tab, a sign-in hops through three addresses before it lands. Your test starts with one tab,
        <code>page</code>, and <code>page</code> stays pointed at it, whatever opens next. Following the user means
        catching the new tab, waiting for it to arrive, and remembering which tab you're acting on.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Catch a tab as it opens</h2>
    <p>
        A new tab opened from your page fires its <code>popup</code> event. Start waiting for it <em>first</em>,
        click, then <code>await</code> the promise:
    </p>
<pre><code>const opening = page.waitForEvent('popup');
await page.getByRole('link', { name: 'Help center' }).click();
const help = await opening;

await expect(help.getByRole('heading', { name: 'How can we help?' })).toBeVisible();</code></pre>
    <p class="tip">
        <strong>Order matters, again.</strong> Like waiting for a response on floor 6, the listening has to start
        before the click. There's no <code>await</code> on the first line: you want the promise, so you can click
        while it listens.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>help</code> is a full <code>Page</code>: locators, <code>expect</code>, screenshots, all of it.
        <code>page</code> is still the tab you started in, so you can go back to it whenever you like, with no
        switching. Done with the new one? <code>await help.close();</code>
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Wait for where you're going</h2>
    <p>
        Some pages send you on as soon as they load: a redirect after sign-in, a "processing…" screen. Reading the
        first page you land on gets you the wrong one. Wait for the address instead:
    </p>
<pre><code>await page.waitForURL('**/order/confirmed');   // a glob
await page.waitForURL(/\/order\/\d+$/);         // or a regex</code></pre>
    <p>
        For moving yourself, there's <code>goto</code>, <code>goBack</code>, <code>goForward</code> and
        <code>reload</code>, the browser's own buttons. Locators and <code>expect</code> wait for the new page on
        their own, so you rarely need more than that.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Every tab in one place</h2>
    <p>
        Tabs belong to a browser context, a private browser profile your test gets to itself.
        <code>page.context().pages()</code> lists every open tab, and the context's <code>page</code> event fires
        for each new one, however it opened. Handy when a site opens something you didn't click, or when you want to
        check that a stray tab didn't appear.
    </p>
<pre><code>expect(page.context().pages()).toHaveLength(2);</code></pre>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Pay the merchant</h2>
    <p>
        This floor's merchant won't say his price in the room. He writes it on the far side of a portal, which opens
        in a new tab, and the portal doesn't drop you at his stall straight away. Read the right sign, then pay where
        you came from. Eight seconds.
    </p>
</section>
